Save and load a state without extra copies of the uncompressed state

A New 3DS state is 296 MB uncompressed: FCRAM 256 MB, VRAM 6 MB, the extra
RAM 4 MB and DSP RAM 512 KB, plus about 30 MB of everything else. It is
compressed to about 20 MB. The core copied the uncompressed bytes around
both ways:

- load (LoadState, LoadStateBuffer): the decompressed vector, a std::string
  copy of it, and the istringstream's own copy of that string were all alive
  at once. LoadStateBuffer also copied the compressed bytes first.
- save (SaveState, SaveStateBuffer): the ostringstream doubled its way up,
  str() copied it whole, and CompressDataZSTD zero-filled a vector of the
  worst-case size (about the source's size), then kept that capacity after
  resize().

On an iPhone 14 Pro (Majora's Mask 3D, 2026-10-19) resuming a game was killed
by the system for memory before its first frame: the app had 640 MB at the
end of the boot, and the load added about 900 MB at its peak. The bench on the
host (Tools/3ds-bench roundtrip, resident size sampled every 0.1 s) showed a
load peak of +900 MB and a save peak of +600 MB over steady play.

This patch:

- deserializes from a stream buffer over the decompressed bytes
  (ByteViewBuffer), with no copy; LoadStateBuffer decompresses from a span of
  the buffer it was given and releases that buffer;
- serializes into a stream buffer that appends to a vector (ByteSinkBuffer),
  reserved at the size of the last state, or for the first state at the size
  of the emulated memories it carries plus 40 MB, so it does not grow; that
  vector is compressed directly and released;
- compresses into an uninitialized scratch buffer of the worst-case size and
  returns a vector of exactly the compressed size. The scratch pages the
  compressor never writes are never committed.

The archive format is unchanged. A state written before this patch
decompresses to the same size with the same header, and loads. With the
patch, both peaks are about +310 MB over steady play (one uncompressed
copy). Bench roundtrip results are unchanged: over 300 frames, the picture
differs only on the first frame after the load, as before, and the sound is
identical.

